  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of the **typical jersey numbers for linebackers** in the NFL:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccb Current NFL Rules (as of 2021 expansion)\n\n**Linebackers** are allowed to wear numbers in the ranges **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc8 Most Common/Traditional Linebacker Number Ranges\n\n| Range | Notes |\n|-------|-------|\n| **50\u201359** | The classic, traditional linebacker numbers |\n| **90\u201399** | Also very common, especially for pass-rushing linebackers/edge rushers |\n| **40\u201349** | Opened up to linebackers more recently |\n| **1\u201339** | Now allowed under the 2021 expansion rules |\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdc How the Rules Evolved\n\n- In **1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the **50\u201359** range.\n\n- The NFL Competition Committee made another change in **2015**, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40 through 49** for the first time.\n\n- In **2021**, NFL owners passed a rule expanding available numbers, which included allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379 and 90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf1f Historically Iconic Linebacker Numbers\n\n- **#56** \u2013 Lawrence Taylor (Giants)\n- **#55** \u2013 Junior Seau, Derrick Thomas\n- **#52** \u2013 Ray Lewis\n- **#58** \u2013 Jack Lambert, Derrick Brooks\n- **#99** \u2013 Luke Kuechly (later), various pass rushers\n\nSo while **50s** remain the most \"classic\" linebacker numbers, you'll just as commonly see linebackers sporting numbers in the **90s** today, and increasingly in the **1\u201349** range as well.",
